  <header class="qa-header">
    <p class="qa-pillar">Compare · CMP·12</p>
    <h1>White-necked Raven vs Pied Crow: How to Tell Them Apart</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Both birds are large black corvids with white markings, but the patterns are completely different: the White-necked Raven has a white patch on the nape and upper back; the Pied Crow has a white chest and belly, with a white collar. The White-necked Raven is also noticeably larger and heavier, with a massive deep-arched bill — immediately distinctive once seen.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Side-by-Side Comparison</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Feature</th><th>White-necked Raven</th><th>Pied Crow</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Scientific name</td><td><em>Corvus albicollis</em></td><td><em>Corvus albus</em></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Body length</td><td>50–54 cm</td><td>46–52 cm</td></tr>
        <tr><td>White on plumage</td><td>Nape, upper back/neck patch</td><td>Breast, belly, collar (front half of underparts)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Rest of plumage</td><td>Glossy black</td><td>Glossy black (back, wings, tail)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Bill</td><td>Very heavy, deeply arched, white tip</td><td>Heavy but less extreme; no white tip</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Tail</td><td>Wedge-shaped (raven)</td><td>Graduated, less wedge-shaped</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Preferred habitat</td><td>Mountains, gorges, rocky cliffs</td><td>Open farmland, savannah, urban areas</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Range</td><td>Mountains of E and S Africa</td><td>Widespread sub-Saharan Africa</td></tr>
        <tr><td>IUCN status</td><td>Least Concern</td><td>Least Concern</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>The White Pattern — the Easiest Separation</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The white markings in the two species do not overlap:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <p><strong>White-necked Raven:</strong> white is confined to the nape and upper back — a patch visible from behind and above, not from the front. From a distance in front view, a White-necked Raven looks all-black.</p>
    <p><strong>Pied Crow:</strong> white is on the breast and belly — a large white "bib" covering the chest and upper belly, with a white collar connecting to white on the lower back. From a front view, a Pied Crow is strikingly bicoloured — clearly not all-black.</p>
    <p>This single character eliminates confusion in most cases. Any large black African corvid with a white chest is a Pied Crow; any with white only on the nape/back is a White-necked Raven.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Bill Size — Dramatic Difference</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The White-necked Raven has one of the most impressive bills of any corvid — very heavy, strongly arched (deep-curved from base to tip), and tipped with white. This bill allows it to open large carcasses and exploit tough food items unavailable to smaller corvids. The Pied Crow has a substantial corvid bill, but it is proportionally much less extreme. Bill profile alone identifies a White-necked Raven at close range.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Related Pages</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/species/white-necked-raven/">White-necked Raven — species profile</a></li>
      <li><a href="/compare/raven-vs-crow/">Raven vs Crow</a></li>
